.TH FATHOM_ATTRIBUTION_CORRECT 1  "fathom 0.1.0-alpha.1" 
.SH NAME
fathom\-attribution\-correct \- Store a correction: these identities were written by an agent / by a person
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfathom attribution correct\fR [\fB\-\-root\fR] <\fB\-\-kind\fR> [\fB\-\-agent\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR] [\fB\-\-identity\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fITARGET\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Store a correction: these identities were written by an agent / by a person
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-root\fR \fI<ROOT>\fR [default: .]
The repository to write to
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fI<KIND>\fR
`agent`, `human` or `unknown`
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fI<AGENT>\fR
Which agent the identity is, when `\-\-kind agent`
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fI<NOTE>\fR
Why the label was overruled, for whoever reads it next
.TP
\fB\-\-identity\fR \fI<IDENTITY>\fR
Correct one identity key directly (`identity:<address>`), instead of deriving them from a commit
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the result as JSON on stdout, and nothing else there
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fITARGET\fR]
The commit whose identities are being corrected: a rev, an oid prefix, or `\-` for nothing
